 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ for single point/pose transformation. $O(N)$ for processing $N$ poses.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space for single transformation. $O(N)$ to store $N$ poses.
-- **Strengths:** Simple, computationally inexpensive, numerically stable for small transformations.
-- **Limitations:** Accumulated error in sequential transformations; not suitable for large continuous motion without integration (see Continuous Camera Motion Modeling).
-- **When to use:** Standard coordinate frame alignment, dataset format conversion (LLFF), initial scene centering.
-- **When not to use:** Large continuous motion modeling (use Continuous Camera Motion Modeling), complex interpolation (use Quaternions).
+- **Time Complexity:** Discrete transformations are $O(1)$ per point/pose. Continuous motion integration is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of time steps, dominated by the MLP evaluation and matrix multiplications.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for discrete poses ($3 \times 4$ matrix). Continuous modeling requires storing the MLP weights and potentially intermediate velocity states.
+- **Strengths:** Provides a flexible framework for handling diverse input formats (COLMAP, LLFF) and motion types (discrete, continuous). Essential for stabilizing NeRF training.
+- **Limitations:** Discrete rotations can suffer from gimbal lock if not implemented carefully (though axis-specific rotations avoid this for single axes). Continuous integration requires careful handling of numerical drift in rotation matrices (orthogonalization) [G].
+- **When to use:** When preparing camera data for volume rendering, converting between coordinate systems, or animating camera paths in video-based NeRF.
 
 ## Background
-Camera Pose Transformation is a fundamental operation in computer graphics and computer vision, rooted in the mathematical representation of rigid body motions via the Special Euclidean group $SE(3)$ [S1]. The representation of poses as $3 \times 4$ matrices or $4 \times 4$ homogeneous matrices is standard in NeRF literature [S1, S5]. The specific application of offset and rotation primitives for scene centering and coordinate alignment is widely used in raymarching and SDF rendering tutorials [S3, S2, unofficial]. The conversion between COLMAP and LLFF formats is a standard preprocessing step for NeRF training on real-world datasets [S5].
+The representation of camera poses using the Lie group $SE(3)$ is standard in computer vision and robotics [S1]. The specific application of these transformations to volume rendering, particularly NeRF, has become critical for handling noisy poses and continuous video data. The LLFF format conversion is specific to the Local Light Field Fusion dataset conventions [S5]. Continuous motion modeling was introduced to address the limitations of discrete pose estimation in monocular video scenarios [S4].
 
